 -- olemd <olemd@glemt.net>  Thu, 12 Nov 2026 11:50:15 +0100

lanwatch (0.9.3) unstable; urgency=medium

  * A config error now fails "systemctl start" and "systemctl restart"
    at once, with the reason in "systemctl status": the service is
    Type=notify and tells systemd when it is up. Before, systemctl
    reported success and the reason was only in the journal.
  * After an upgrade, the package checks /etc/lanwatch/config.toml and
    prints why the service will not start, if it will not.
  * An unknown key is reported as "unknown setting" with the version,
    instead of pydantic's "Extra inputs are not permitted".
  * Running lanwatch without sudo on a packaged install says the config
    file cannot be read, instead of printing a traceback.

 -- olemd <olemd@glemt.net>  Thu, 12 Nov 2026 10:34:28 +0100

lanwatch (0.9.2) unstable; urgency=medium

  * One ups.power-event finding per outage: the start is worked out on
    the outage's first read and kept while the UPS stays on battery.
    Reads that drifted across a 10-minute boundary, or a UPS that does
    not report its time on battery, used to open several.
  * junos.config-changed findings resolve by themselves after 7 days
    instead of waiting to be acknowledged; the timeline keeps every
    commit.

 -- olemd <olemd@glemt.net>  Thu, 12 Nov 2026 10:05:23 +0100

lanwatch (0.9.1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * [snmp] enabled is the switch for all of SNMP again: false reads none
    of the devices and checks no logins. enabled = true left over from
    0.8.0 next to device tables no longer fails with a password error.
  * A device listed at the router's default-gateway address is read once,
    as the router, instead of overwriting the router's state.
  * SNMP default-credential findings stay open when a device misses one
    weekly probe: results now age out after three deep-scan intervals,
    not 10 days.
  * The UPS card's firmware is shown as the newest known, with the date
    the list of releases was last checked.
  * SNMP times without a zone are read as local time everywhere; the
    Junos reader took them as UTC.

 -- olemd <olemd@glemt.net>  Thu, 12 Nov 2026 09:02:37 +0100

lanwatch (0.9.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * SNMP for any number of devices: one [snmp.devices.<name>] table per
    device, each with its own SNMPv3 login; router = true marks the one
    whose ARP table and leases are read. 0.8.0's form, the router's login
    straight under [snmp] with enabled = true, is still read as a device
    named router, and its state moves over on upgrade (#11).
  * Checks for every SNMP device: a clock that is off (snmp.clock-skew)
    and a device that stops answering (snmp.unreachable) (#12).
  * UPS over SNMP (UPS-MIB, and PowerNet on APC cards): state, charge,
    runtime, load, voltages, battery and self-test on the device page,
    graphs over a day and a week, a dashboard tile, and seven rules for
    lost communication, running on battery, low or worn-out battery,
    alarms, an old self-test and power events (#13).
  * Junos extras for Juniper gear read over SNMP, with nothing to set up:
    chassis alarms, full storage, FRUs not online, a full session table,
    config commits, a missing rescue config and rising DHCP declines
    (#14).
  * SNMP default-credential probe ([snmp_audit], on by default): with
    the weekly deep scan and for each new device, one GET over SNMPv1
    and v2c with public and private, and whether an SNMPv3 agent answers.
    A default community that answers is a finding, critical for private.
    scan.skip_hosts are left out. "lanwatch snmp-audit [HOST]" runs it
    now (#15).
  * Links between devices that belong together: a BMC (iLO, iDRAC, IPMI)
    and its server, a device and its switch, a device and its UPS. Added
    by hand or from suggestions on the device page; a watched server
    that is off while its BMC answers says so (#18).
  * Virtual machines are marked by their hypervisor's MAC prefix (#19).
  * scan.resolve_hostnames now applies to every discovery backend, not
    only the ping sweep (#17).
  * The device page no longer says the firmware is behind when it is
    current (#16).

 -- olemd <olemd@glemt.net>  Thu, 12 Nov 2026 00:01:27 +0100

lanwatch (0.8.1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * KoboldCpp as an LLM backend next to Ollama: set llm.backend =
    "koboldcpp" and point llm.base_url at the instance (port 5001 by
    default). KoboldCpp serves the one model it was started with and its
    context size is set at launch, so llm.model is only a label there
    and num_ctx, chat_num_ctx and keep_alive are ignored.
    Password-protected instances are not supported. The default is still
    Ollama.
  * A second LLM server as fallback ([llm.fallback] with backend,
    base_url and model), used when the first cannot be reached or has no
    model. A slow or failing answer does not switch servers. Answers are
    cached under the model that wrote them.
  * "lanwatch llm check" and the status page name the backend and show
    both servers; the check fails only when neither is usable.
  * An LLM host that is switched off is reported as unreachable instead
    of as a timeout.

 -- olemd <olemd@glemt.net>  Wed, 11 Nov 2026 13:32:27 +0100

lanwatch (0.8.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Router over SNMPv3 ([snmp], read-only, off by default): what the
    router is, its ARP and IPv6 neighbour table and, on Juniper, its DHCP
    leases, every infra round or with "lanwatch snmp". Each device shows
    the interface the router reaches it on and whether it holds a lease;
    the infrastructure page has the whole table. The tables are never
    taken as presence: addresses the router knows but the scan missed get
    the recheck's slower ping. Authentication and encryption only, no
    v1/v2c communities. Needs snmpbulkwalk; the package now recommends
    snmp. See docs/snmp.md.
  * New rule net.router-arp-mismatch: the router ties an address to
    another MAC than the one that answered lanwatch, two reads in a row.
    Critical for the gateway, DHCP and DNS addresses.
  * airMAX radios ([airos], read-only, off by default): model, airOS
    version and the link each radio is part of, with signal, noise, CCQ
    and airMAX quality from both ends, every infra round or with
    "lanwatch airos". Uses the radio's read-only account over HTTPS, with
    the certificate pinned on first contact; "lanwatch airos --accept-cert
    HOST" pins a new one. One login attempt per round and none after a
    rejected password until the configured credentials change, because
    airOS locks the account after three failures; "lanwatch airos
    --retry-login HOST" tries once more when the account was fixed on the
    radio instead. The firmware check covers the radios. See
    docs/airos.md.
  * Six new rules for the radios: airos.link-down, airos.link-poor,
    airos.link-degraded (worse than the link's own learned normal, which
    is kept for about a week while the link is down),
    airos.channel-overlap (a 2.4 GHz link on top of Wi-Fi channels),
    airos.certificate-changed and airos.login-rejected.
  * The chat's rule list carries the first sentence of each description,
    so the whole list fits in one tool result.

 -- olemd <olemd@glemt.net>  Tue, 10 Nov 2026 17:03:55 +0100

lanwatch (0.7.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * The scheduler runs jobs in lanes. Service scans (inventory, deep) have
    their own lane and so do the LLM jobs (digest, suggestions), so
    discovery and the infra probes keep their schedule during a long scan;
    on a fresh install they used to wait behind the first inventory and
    deep scan. Within a lane jobs still run one at a time. The header
    indicator lists every running job.
  * Discovery takes a second, slower look at addresses that stayed silent
    but should be there: devices that were online in the previous round
    and, with [unifi] enabled, clients the controller lists on a scanned
    subnet. Three pings with a two-second wait; only a reply counts. A
    power-saving Wi-Fi device that sleeps through the scan is no longer
    reported offline and back online. New setting scan.recheck_silent, on
    by default.
  * New setting scan.skip_hosts: devices (IP or MAC) that are discovered
    and tracked like the rest but never port-scanned, for gear that crashes
    or reboots under nmap. Empty by default.
  * The infrastructure page tells apart "not probed yet", "the DHCP probe
    needs root, CAP_NET_RAW or the helper" and "no DHCP server answered";
    it used to blame missing privileges before the first infra round.
  * UniFi: the offline finding names the model once ("UniFi USW Pro Max 24
    is offline"), "lanwatch unifi" sizes its device columns to their
    contents, and the documentation covers UniFi OS Server and where the
    API key is created in Network 10.

 -- olemd <olemd@glemt.net>  Tue, 10 Nov 2026 10:02:59 +0100

lanwatch (0.6.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * UniFi controller ([unifi], read-only, API key): per client the uplink
    access point or switch, wired/wireless, guest access, the controller's
    name and the network/VLAN; the networks, SSIDs and UniFi devices with
    state, ports, radios and pending firmware. New rules
    net.iot-on-main-network, inventory.unifi-only and
    net.unifi-device-offline; the flat-network note groups by the
    controller's networks. A Topology page draws gateway, UniFi devices and
    clients as a tree, with the same tree as a list. "lanwatch unifi" reads
    the controller on demand. See /etc/lanwatch/config.toml.default and
    /usr/share/doc/lanwatch/unifi.md.
  * /etc/lanwatch/config.toml is now a comment-only stub that will not
    change between versions, so upgrades stop asking about it. The full
    reference with every setting lives next to it as config.toml.default,
    refreshed on each upgrade, and the upgrade lists settings that are new
    since the previous version. This upgrade asks once more about the old
    file; answer N to keep yours.
  * "lanwatch config show" prints *** for a set API key, token or password.
  * Firmware findings read "has an update available" when the latest
    version is not known, as for UniFi devices.

