lanwatch (0.5.1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Discovery no longer fails when [latency] targets names an address
    outside the local networks (such as 1.1.1.1): the helper refused the
    ping and the error took the whole round down. Pings now run from the
    service itself, which needs no privilege, and a target that cannot be
    measured is logged and skipped.

 -- olemd <olemd@glemt.net>  Mon, 09 Nov 2026 12:33:15 +0100

lanwatch (0.5.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Latency: a few pings to the gateway every discovery round. The dashboard
    shows the round-trip time with a day's trend, and sustained packet loss
    becomes a finding (net.packet-loss). Add an internet address under
    [latency] targets to measure the path out as well.
  * Internet exposure (opt-in, [exposure]): a daily lookup of the public
    address in Shodan's InternetDB. Ports that answer from the internet are
    a finding, critical when Shodan matched the service to published
    vulnerabilities. Shown on the infrastructure page; "lanwatch exposure"
    runs it by hand. Off by default: it tells Shodan the public address.
  * Presence: every device page shows a heatmap of the hours and weekdays
    the device answered over the last four weeks, and names the hour it is
    most often away.
  * Randomised MAC addresses: a new device with a private (locally
    administered) address is reported at low severity, and a note explains
    why it may reappear as a new device. A prefix that no manufacturer
    holds in the IEEE registry is a separate low finding.
  * Home Assistant: with notify.home_assistant.lanwatch_url set, tapping a
    notification opens lanwatch, and a single new finding gets an "Accept"
    button ("Yes, that's mine" for a new device). docs/home-assistant.md,
    installed with the package, covers the token, the sensors, the buttons
    and troubleshooting.

 -- olemd <olemd@glemt.net>  Mon, 09 Nov 2026 12:11:09 +0100

lanwatch (0.4.4) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Devices the scanner could not name now get their vendor from the IEEE
    registry: arp-scan ships an OUI list from March 2024, so every prefix
    assigned since (e4:65:b8, Espressif, among many) showed no vendor. The
    registry is downloaded weekly by a new "oui" job; with [oui] online =
    false, nmap's installed copy is used instead.
  * Firmware and OUI online lookups are on by default. Both fetch public
    lists and send nothing about the network; online = false turns them off.

